<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-1-1-overview" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Chapter Overview</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Welcome to computer programming! Learning to program in Java is one of the most
      practical and empowering skills you can develop, giving you the ability to instruct
      a computer to solve complex problems, automate repetitive tasks, and bring creative
      ideas to life.
    </p>

    <p>
      However, writing computer software is very different from using software as an
      end-user. When you click an icon or tap an app on your phone, the underlying
      mechanics are hidden behind polished interfaces. As a software developer, you can
      no longer afford to treat the computer as a magic black box. If you try to learn
      programming purely as an exercise in memorizing Java syntax rules, you will
      inevitably hit walls of frustration when things do not work as expected.
    </p>

    <p>
      To become a confident, effective programmer, you need a mental model of what is
      physically taking place beneath the surface when your code runs. That foundation
      begins right here in Chapter 1.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-1-1-1-demystifying-machine">
    <title>Demystifying the Machine: Hardware &amp; the Fetch-and-Execute Cycle</title>

    <p>
      Before looking at a single line of Java, we will explore how computer hardware
      functions. At its heart, a Central Processing Unit (CPU) is an astonishingly fast
      but remarkably literal calculator. It does not understand high-level concepts like
      <q>draw a button</q> or <q>calculate sales tax.</q> Instead, it operates in a
      continuous, rhythmic heartbeat known as the fetch-and-execute cycle: retrieving
      microscopic machine-language instructions from memory, decoding them, and carrying
      them out step by step.
    </p>

    <p>
      Understanding this hardware cycle clarifies:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>Why computers require instructions that are completely unambiguous.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>How programs represent data using binary switches (bits and bytes).</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          How main memory (RAM) is organized and why managing memory effectively is
          central to programming.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      When you understand how a CPU reads and processes memory, programming stops feeling
      like an abstract puzzle and becomes an intuitive process of directing real hardware.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-1-1-2-java-solution-jvm">
    <title>The Java Solution: Bytecode and the Java Virtual Machine (JVM)</title>

    <p>
      Different physical computers speak different native machine languages; a processor in
      an Intel-based Windows PC does not understand the binary instructions built for an
      Apple silicon Mac or an Android smartphone. Historically, this meant software written
      for one machine had to be rewritten or recompiled for every other platform.
    </p>

    <p>
      Java revolutionized software development by introducing a brilliant layer of
      abstraction: the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).
    </p>

    <p>
      In this chapter, you will learn how the Java compiler translates your human-readable
      source code into an intermediate format called Java bytecode. Instead of running
      directly on your physical processor, this bytecode is executed by the JVM. Because
      the JVM acts as a universal interpreter running on top of any operating system, Java
      delivers on its famous promise: <q>Write Once, Run Anywhere.</q> Understanding the
      relationship between your source code, the compiler, and the JVM will give you the
      clarity needed to navigate builds and runtime environments throughout your programming
      journey.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-1-1-3-setting-up-workshop">
    <title>Setting Up Your Workshop: The JDK, the Command Line, and Eclipse</title>

    <p>
      A craftsperson needs both an understanding of their materials and mastery of their tools.
      The second half of this chapter is hands-on: you will configure your professional
      software development environment.
    </p>

    <p>
      We will walk through:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          The Java Development Kit (JDK): Installing the official tools—including the Java
          compiler (<c>javac</c>) and the runtime launcher (<c>java</c>)—that allow your
          computer to build and run Java applications.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The Command Line &amp; JShell: Compiling and executing a classic <q>Hello, World!</q>
          program directly from your system’s terminal. Working at the command line strips away
          unnecessary distractions, letting you see the direct relationship between <c>.java</c>
          source files and <c>.class</c> bytecode files. You will also meet JShell, Java's
          interactive Read-Eval-Print Loop (REPL), which provides an instant scratchpad for
          experimenting with code snippets.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The Eclipse IDE: Graduating to a modern Integrated Development Environment (IDE). An
          IDE combines a smart code editor, compiler, file manager, and debugger into a single
          workspace. You will learn how to create a project, write your code, and run programs
          seamlessly within Eclipse.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      By the end of this chapter, you will understand how instructions flow from your keyboard
      into memory and through the CPU, your tools will be installed and configured, and you
      will have written and executed your very first Java program. With this foundation in
      place, you will be fully prepared to dive into Chapter 2 and begin programming!
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>